---
title: Open5GS and UERANSIM
---

# Open5GS and UERANSIM: two simulated UEs

This disposable lab registers two UEs, establishes their PDU sessions, and sends
traffic through both UE tunnels. No radio hardware is required. This does not
validate a production deployment, legacy EPC, or every Kubernetes distribution.

Validated: **@VALIDATED_AT@**, Open5GS chart **@OPEN5GS_VERSION@**, UERANSIM chart
**@UERANSIM_VERSION@**. [Validation run](@RUN_URL@).

## Prerequisites

Use a Linux x86-64 host with Docker, Kind 0.33.0, kubectl 1.37 and Helm 4.3,
Bash, Python 3, curl and Git. Allow at least 4 CPUs and 8 GiB available memory
for the lab. The automated reference environment is Ubuntu 22.04 on a GitHub
hosted runner. The host kernel must support SCTP and TUN; MongoDB 8.0 must also
support that kernel. The runner uses the Ubuntu 22.04 kernel line.

The host needs outbound HTTPS to pull packages/images and outbound ICMP to
8.8.8.8 for the connectivity checks. The simulated UE pod is privileged: use
only a disposable lab cluster where this is permitted. MongoDB is ephemeral;
the subscriber keys in these values are public test credentials. Do not use
these values to upgrade an existing database.

## Get the exact tested instructions

```bash
git clone https://github.com/Gradiant/5g-charts.git
cd 5g-charts
git checkout @COMMIT@
export KUBECONFIG="$PWD/reports/tutorial-kubeconfig"
mkdir -p reports
kind create cluster --name 5g-tutorial --wait 120s \
  --image kindest/node:v1.37.0@sha256:a1ed56cfb0e7b93589bdf97c8cd566405a265939e3620fc4f5de89adff580ae5 \
  --kubeconfig "$KUBECONFIG"
```

Keep this kubeconfig in the same shell for every following command. Use a new
cluster: the scripts refuse contexts other than `kind-5g-tutorial`.

## Install the published charts

```bash
bash tutorials/open5gs-ueransim/tutorial.sh install
```

The [script](https://github.com/Gradiant/5g-charts/blob/@COMMIT@/tutorials/open5gs-ueransim/tutorial.sh)
downloads both charts from `oci://registry-1.docker.io/gradiantcharts` at the
versions in `versions.json`, then uses `helm upgrade --install --wait` with the
two checked-in values files. Those are the same commands used in validation.

- [Open5GS values](docs/open5gs-ueransim-gnb/5gSA-values.yaml): enable WebUI and
  subscriber population, disable legacy 4G functions, use PLMN 999/70 and slice
  SST 1 / SD 111111, and create subscribers 999700000000001 and 999700000000002.
- [UERANSIM values](docs/open5gs-ueransim-gnb/gnb-ues-values.yaml): launch a gNB
  and two matching UEs. All releases use namespace `open5gs-tutorial`; the AMF
  Service name is `open5gs-amf-ngap`.

## Verify the complete connection

```bash
bash tutorials/open5gs-ueransim/tutorial.sh verify
```

This requires separate successful registration and PDU-session log entries for
each UE, addresses on `uesimtun0` and `uesimtun1`, and five successful pings to
8.8.8.8 through each tunnel (zero packet loss). It also queries MongoDB using
`mongosh`, adds and verifies a third subscriber, and checks the WebUI HTTP page.
Pods being Ready alone is not sufficient.

For interactive access, run in another terminal with the same kubeconfig:

```bash
kubectl -n open5gs-tutorial port-forward service/open5gs-webui 9999:9999
```

Open http://localhost:9999. Automated validation checks HTTP access, not the
interactive login or subscriber form. Those are not claimed as validated steps.

On a failure, preserve the environment while inspecting the reports:

```bash
bash tutorials/open5gs-ueransim/tutorial.sh diagnostics
```

Logs and evidence are stored under `reports/tutorial-ueransim/`. A failed check
exits nonzero; do not treat a partly working deployment as a successful run.

## Clean up

```bash
bash tutorials/open5gs-ueransim/tutorial.sh clean
kind delete cluster --name 5g-tutorial
```

The first command uninstalls both releases, verifies their pods are gone and
records the validation result. The second deletes the disposable cluster and
its ephemeral database. It does not delete any other cluster.
